**Parent to child: props.** Data flows down. The parent passes values as attributes, and the child receives them in its `props` argument (read-only).

    function Parent() { return <Child name="Asha" />; }
    function Child({ name }) { return <p>Hello {name}</p>; }

**Child to parent: callback props.** The parent keeps the state and passes a function down; the child calls it with the data.

    function Parent() {
      const [msg, setMsg] = useState("");
      return <Child onSend={setMsg} />;
    }
    function Child({ onSend }) {
      return <button onClick={() => onSend("hi")}>Send</button>;
    }

If siblings need the same data, "lift state up" to the nearest common parent.

**Virtual DOM.** A lightweight JavaScript copy of the UI tree. When state changes, React builds a new virtual tree, **diffs** it against the previous one (reconciliation), and updates only the real DOM nodes that changed. This batches changes and avoids rewriting the whole page. Keys on list items help the diff identify items.

**Hooks.** Functions that let function components use state and lifecycle features, such as `useState`, `useEffect`, `useContext`, `useRef`, `useMemo`. Rules: call them only at the top level of a component (not in loops or conditions) and only from components or custom hooks.

**`useState`** returns a state value and its setter: `const [count, setCount] = useState(0);`. Calling the setter schedules a re-render with the new value. State should be treated as immutable (create new arrays and objects).

**`useContext`** reads a value from a React Context, so deeply nested components can access shared data (theme, current user) without passing props through every level (prop drilling). Create it with `createContext`, provide it with `<Ctx.Provider value={...}>`, and read it with `useContext(Ctx)`. Every consumer re-renders when the value changes, so use it for fairly stable data, not rapidly changing state.
